  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1157 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nA stationary front remains draped along the Tennessee /\nMississippi border late Friday evening. This boundary will begin\nto lift northeast as a warm front overnight, allowing a weak\nshortwave to move through the Mid-South. Scattered showers and\nstorms will develop shortly before sunrise, mainly over the\nMississippi Delta region. At this time, forecast soundings \ndepict skinny CAPE profiles with PWATs near the 99th percentile.\nWhile isolated flooding cannot be ruled out, an overall lack of\nsufficient lift and the spotty nature of precipitation will\nnegate the need for a Flood Watch in our area. Rainfall totals\nwill vary from southwest to northeast with the greatest amounts\nnearing 2 inches in the Delta. Locations closer to Kentucky have\nthe greatest chance of remaining dry on Saturday.\n\nHot and humid conditions resume on Sunday as the eastern edge of\na 598 dam ridge builds into the region. Afternoon highs will top\nout in the low to mid 90s with triple digit heat indices\nanticipated areawide. A Heat Advisory will likely be needed for\nareas along and west of the Mississippi River on Sunday.\nTemperatures will soar into the upper 90s to low 100s by Tuesday.\nThis, combined with elevated dewpoints, will produce heat indices\nin excess of 110 degrees. An Extreme Heat Warning is not out of\nthe question for early next week. The latter half of the week\nwill feature a backdoor cold front, decreasing highs to the low\nand mid 90s. While providing minimal relief, this will reduce\nheat indices below headline criteria for most of the Mid-South by\nThursday.\n\nOne tricky aspect to next week's forecast will be the potential\nfor showers and storms. The most recent guidance keeps the Mid-\nSouth dry through midweek. However, northwest flow aloft will\nmake the region susceptible to weak shortwave disturbances\nrotating along the outer periphery of the ridge. This may\nencourage stray shower and storm development in the afternoons,\nparticularly on Wednesday as the aforementioned boundary pushes\ninto the region. For now though, expect a hot and mostly dry end\nof July.\n\nANS",
